ng both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=e^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+1y) & =e^{x}\\cdot 6 \\\\ e^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+e^{x}y & =6 e^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{x}) & =6 e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6 e^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ ye^{x} & =6\\left(1 e^{x}\\right)+C \\\\ ye^{x} & =6 e^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\left(C + 6 e^{x}\\right) e^{- x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =\\frac{(6 e^{(0)})+C}{(e^{(0)})} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{6+C}{1} \\\\ C & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6 - 9 e^{- x}.\n$$" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495407 1080 "e^{- 6 x}" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495407 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495407 1083 #uuid "424828d0-b396-5446-bbd3-769e09edb5c4" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495408 1080 "-3" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495408 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495408 1083 #uuid "42b1d7a7-d499-5b92-ad06-1a13338c37d9" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495428 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495449 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495409 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\left(6\\right)y=6 x e^{- 6 x},\\quad y(0)=6\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495409 1072 #uuid "43867ed7-bd35-5123-ad3b-db1c0f0e8adf" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495409 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=6$ and $Q(x)=6 x e^{- 6 x}.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{6 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=e^{6 x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e^{6 x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y) & =e^{6 x}\\cdot 6 x e^{- 6 x} \\\\ e^{6 x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6 e^{6 x}y & =6 x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{6 x}) & =6 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{6 x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6 x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ ye^{6 x} & =3 x^{2}+C \\\\ y & =\\left(C + 3 x^{2}\\right) e^{- 6 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =\\frac{(3 (0)^{2})+C}{(e^{6 (0)})} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{0+C}{1} \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3 \\left(x^{2} + 2\\right) e^{- 6 x}.\n$$" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495419 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495491 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495410 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\nx\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=24 x^{2},\\quad x>0,\\quad y(1)=-6\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495410 1072 #uuid "440a15bb-8981-5d44-b0fa-73854b5eaffc" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495410 1074 "The first step is to write the equation in standard form,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y & =24 x^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}\\left(x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\right) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot 24 x^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y & =24 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=24 x.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{\\int \\frac{2}{x}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}} \\\\  & =x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=x^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y) & =x^{2}\\cdot 24 x \\\\ x^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2 xy & =24 x^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx^{2}) & =24 x^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 24 x^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yx^{2} & =6 x^{4}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{C + 6 x^{4}}{x^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =\\frac{(6 (1)^{4})+C}{((1)^{2})} \\\\ -6 & =\\frac{6+C}{1} \\\\ C & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{6 \\left(x^{4} - 2\\right)}{x^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495411 1080 "5 e^{\\frac{x^{3}}{9}} + 1" 13194139535323 17592186045443 true] [17592186495411 1082 #edb/ref 1759218604